{"from": "passage:mk-10-38", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-20-22", "note": "The same cup-and-baptism question and answer."}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-39", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:mt-20-22-the-cup"}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-42", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:mt-20-25-rulers-lord-it-over"}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-42", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-20-25", "note": "The rulers of the nations lord it over their subjects."}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-43", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:mk-10-43-44-great-servant"}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-43", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-20-26", "note": "Whoever wants to become great shall be your servant; Mark adds 'bondservant of all' at v44."}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-44", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:mk-10-43-44-great-servant"}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-45", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:mt-20-28-ransom-for-many"}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-45", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-20-28", "note": "The identical ransom saying: the Son of Man came not to be served but to serve."}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-46", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:mt-20-29-out-of-jericho"}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-46", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:mk-10-46-47-bartimaeus"}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-46", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-20-29", "note": "Leaving Jericho on the way up to Jerusalem."}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-47", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:mk-10-46-47-bartimaeus"}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-47", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-20-30", "note": "The blind man(s) by the road crying 'son of David, have mercy on me'; Mark names one blind beggar, Bartimaeus son of Timaeus, Matthew two unnamed blind men."}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-48", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:mk-10-46-47-bartimaeus"}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-51", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:mk-10-51-52-sight-restored"}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-51", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-20-33", "note": "The healing of the blind at Jericho; Mark has Bartimaeus address 'Rabboni', Matthew has the two men ask directly."}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-52", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:mk-10-51-52-sight-restored"}
{"from": "passage:mk-10-52", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-20-34", "note": "Their eyes opened and they followed him; Mark adds that Bartimaeus followed 'on the way'."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-gittin-9-10", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-10-02-divorce-question", "grade": "C", "note": "Mishnah Gittin 9:10 preserves the dispute between the Houses of Shammai and Hillel over the grounds for divorce - the lawfulness of divorce was a live, disputed question. Redacted c. 200 CE; it may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly."}
{"from": "text:damascus-document-4-20-5-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-10-11-adultery-no-exception", "grade": "C", "note": "The Damascus Document forbids taking a second wife while the first lives - remarriage while a spouse lived was treated as a serious offense at Qumran. It attests a strict view of the custom in the period, not Mark's exact rule."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-15-259", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-10-12-wife-divorce", "grade": "B", "note": "Salome 'sent him a bill of divorce' and dissolved her marriage with Costobarus - a named woman divorcing her husband in Herod's Judea, the same period and place. It attests the practice, not Jesus's saying."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-15-259", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:mk-10-12-wife-divorce", "note": "The same passage states she divorced him 'though this was not according to the Jewish laws' - the source that attests the practice states Jewish law did not sanction it."}
{"from": "artifact:elephantine-marriage-contracts", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-10-12-wife-divorce", "grade": "D", "note": "The Elephantine archives of the Judean military colony (5th century BCE) include divorce documents - context for divorce law, not the 1st-century practice, and far earlier. Unprovenanced: the papyri surfaced on the local antiquities grey market from the late 19th century, so their authenticity cannot be secured."}
{"from": "artifact:yehohanan-heel-bone", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-10-21-take-up-cross", "grade": "C", "note": "The crucified man's heel bone from Giv'at ha-Mivtar attests 1st-century crucifixion practice in Judea - the brutal custom behind the 'take up the cross' idiom. It does not attest the saying or its metaphorical use."}
{"from": "text:psalms-of-solomon-17", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-10-46-47-bartimaeus", "grade": "C", "note": "Psalms of Solomon 17 asks God to raise up 'their king, the son of David' - the 'son of David' royal-expectation idiom was current in Judea. It attests the acclamation's background, not Bartimaeus or the cry."}
{"from": "site:herodian-jericho-palaces", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-10-46-47-bartimaeus", "grade": "C", "note": "The Herodian winter palaces at Jericho (Tulul Abu el-'Alayiq) confirm Jericho as the Herodian city on the road to Jerusalem in the 1st century - the setting of the scene, not the beggar. Regraded B to C by architect review: a grade covers the whole claim; the palaces confirm the Jericho setting, not the named beggar or his cry."}
{"from": "observation:jerusalem-jericho-ascent", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-10-46-47-bartimaeus", "grade": "C", "note": "Jericho lies far below Jerusalem on the ascent road - the road by which travellers went up from Jericho; context for the road-side scene."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-15-259", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikisource-ant15-salome"}
{"from": "artifact:elephantine-marriage-contracts", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-elephantine-papyri"}
{"from": "claim:mk-10-12-wife-divorce", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-women-in-judaism"}
{"from": "claim:mk-10-02-divorce-question", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-19-03-divorce-question", "note": "The same test question; Mark asks it without Matthew's qualifier 'for any cause' (Matthew 19:3)."}
{"from": "claim:mk-10-11-adultery-no-exception", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-19-09-divorce-adultery", "note": "Mark states the adultery rule without Matthew's 'except for sexual immorality' exception and adds 'against her'."}
{"from": "claim:mk-10-12-wife-divorce", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-19-09-divorce-adultery", "note": "Mark extends the adultery rule to a woman who divorces her husband and remarries; Matthew addresses only the man's divorce and remarriage."}
{"from": "claim:mk-10-19-do-not-defraud", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-19-18-commandments", "note": "Mark adds 'Do not defraud' to the commandment list; Matthew's list (19:18-19) does not have it."}
{"from": "claim:mk-10-21-take-up-cross", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-19-21-sell-give-poor", "note": "The same 'sell, give to the poor, follow me' saying; Mark adds 'taking up the cross' where Matthew 19:21 reads simply 'come, follow me'."}
{"from": "claim:mk-10-35-sons-request", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-20-20-21-mother-request", "note": "The same request: Matthew tells it as made by the mother of the sons of Zebedee with her sons; Mark by the two sons themselves. A difference between Gospels, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:mk-10-43-44-great-servant", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mk-09-35-first-servant", "note": "Mark repeats the saying in a second setting (9:35 at Capernaum; 10:43-44 on the way up to Jerusalem); Matthew has it at 20:26-27."}
{"from": "claim:mk-10-46-47-bartimaeus", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-20-30-31-cry-son-of-david", "note": "The same acclamation at the same place; Mark names one blind beggar, Bartimaeus son of Timaeus (Mark only), Matthew two unnamed blind men."}
